WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.013
- What if I told you

00:00:01.013 --> 00:00:03.220
that Taylor Swift changed music forever?

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:06.220
And no, I don't mean by
pioneering her own brand

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:08.400
of folksy, yet accessible pop rock.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.210
I mean that she actually changed the way

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:13.000
that music gets made and the
way that artists are able

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.200
to monetize the art that they create.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:16.780
It's actually true.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:19.430
When people look back
on this age of music,

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:22.210
they're going to think about
the Before Taylor Swift Age

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:23.970
and the After Taylor Swift Age.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:26.300
Because, as she found out the hard way,

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:28.570
being a rock star comes
with a lot of perks,

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:30.356
but there's also a dark side.

00:00:30.356 --> 00:00:33.401
♪ Whatever time the deed took place ♪

00:00:33.401 --> 00:00:35.960
♪ Macavity wasn't there ♪

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:37.970
No, not that dark.

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:39.650
But some of the most legendary names

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:41.950
in music have been
screwed by terrible deals

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:43.140
with recording companies.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.390
Bad contracts and greedy
labels have left artists

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.270
like Rick James broke while
companies made hundreds

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:50.750
of millions of dollars on their art.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.180
And Taylor Swift's most
recent album, "Fearless,"

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:56.470
Taylor's version, arrived
at #1 on the Billboard 200

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:58.710
and promptly broke a
bunch of industry records.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.090
This is nothing new for Swift.

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.130
She's been a star since
she was a teenager.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.430
But it's the way that she
achieved these milestones

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:05.530
that's worth talking about.

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:08.040
When Taylor lost control
of the music copyrights

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.060
over her first six
albums, she acted swiftly.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.080
Yes, there's gonna be a
lot of puns in this one.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.830
She acted swiftly by
re-recording her first album.

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:18.850
But why would any musician
rerecord their album

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:20.820
in a way that is almost indistinguishable

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:21.830
from the first one?

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:23.890
Well, we can look to another example.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:26.460
Like Swift, Prince signed
his first music deal

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:27.293
as a teenager.

00:01:27.293 --> 00:01:30.360
He gave Warner Bros. ownership
over his entire back catalog.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:33.200
Prince eventually sued and
changed his name to a symbol

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.910
to try and stop the company
from profiting on his name

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:38.420
and eventually hoarded his
music to prevent corporations

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:39.760
from taking control of his work.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:41.630
Similarly, Paul McCartney lost control

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:43.600
of the Beatles' catalog in the 1960s

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.460
and has worked for
decades to get the rights

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:47.030
to his original Beatles recordings,

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:49.060
and that project is still ongoing.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:50.290
And in this context,

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.550
Swift's bold decision
to rerecord her albums

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:54.890
and reclaim the value of her own music

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:57.360
will influence every
artist of her generation

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.427
and for generations to come.

00:01:59.427 --> 00:02:02.630
(stately music)

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:04.160
Hey, Legal Eagles, it's time to think

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:06.230
like a greedy music label executive

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:08.600
or I guess an entrepreneurial musician.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:11.180
Lisa "Left Eye" Lopez once
described the music industry

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.500
as "a cutthroat business
full of greedy individuals

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.190
who take advantage of young artists."

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:16.023
She would know.

00:02:16.023 --> 00:02:17.730
TLC was one of the biggest acts in music

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:19.450
when they were forced
to file for bankruptcy

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:21.180
because their original
recording deal got them

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.650
just 7% on the sale of each album,

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:25.840
minus deductions for
touring and promotion.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:27.440
Understanding music law is essential

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:29.270
for any musician or songwriter,

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:32.260
but it's not like there's
a school of music law.

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:33.720
And this is daunting for most people,

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.350
who usually have no experience in the law

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:37.060
or drafting legal contracts

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:38.570
when they get started in business.

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:40.640
And in the US, the music
industry is governed

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:43.340
by the Copyright Act, but it
also operates by tradition,

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:46.060
particularly in the music
publishing side of the business.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:48.160
So to understand what is so monumental

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:51.490
about what Taylor Swift has
done and is continuing to do,

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:52.530
we have to learn a little bit

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:54.980
about copyright law
and the music industry.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:56.240
Now, to help understand how this works,

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:57.450
let's talk about a hypothetical.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.700
Let's say you're a
promising singer-songwriter.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:02.240
You write your own music and
record it in your own studio.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:04.000
But how do you get paid for your music?

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.210
Well, it should be simple, right?

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:06.990
You write a song, record it, release it

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:08.710
as a digital download on your website

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:10.730
or on a CD or a cassette tape

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:13.070
or an LP if you're a dirty hipster,

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:14.730
and you get paid when someone buys it.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:16.500
You get 100% of that money.

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:17.333
Yay!

00:03:17.333 --> 00:03:19.880
However, your ability to market
your own songs is limiting.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:21.100
How will people discover you?

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.250
Where will they buy your stuff?

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:23.870
How do you get your songs on the radio?

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:25.400
How will you afford to tour?

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:27.020
What if you think your music would be good

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:28.410
in commercials or movies?

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:30.970
Well, this is where labels
and music publishers

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:32.630
have traditionally entered the picture.

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:36.270
So congrats, the Legal Eagle
Music Label Screaming Eagle

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:37.940
wants to sign you for a record deal.

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:40.230
I'm gonna give you $100,000 in advance.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:42.510
You'll also assign 50%
of your publishing rights

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:45.300
to Screaming Eagle Music
Publishing Co., which I own.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.220
Just sign this legally binding contract

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.360
and you'll be well on
your way to superstardom,

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:51.450
and tomorrow, $100,000 will be transferred

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:52.283
to your bank account.

00:03:52.283 --> 00:03:53.470
Sounds pretty good, right?

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:54.560
Awesome. Awesome.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.120
Awesome. Awes, awes, awesome.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:56.953
But wait!

00:03:56.953 --> 00:03:59.490
Before you say yes or no, you
need to read the contract,

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:02.040
and that's actually not what
happened with Brad Paisley,

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:04.210
because Sony refused to
give Brad Paisley data

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:06.920
on how many albums he sold
because a contract footnote said

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.980
he couldn't challenge
royalty payments until 2006.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:11.490
He couldn't legally get the numbers.

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:13.350
So the fine print
matters, and in that case,

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:15.880
he was probably shortchanged
over $10 million.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:17.110
It all starts with knowing the difference

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:19.030
between two different
copyrights that are involved

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:20.960
in the creation of music, and side note,

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:23.500
there's actually at least
six different copyrights

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:24.530
that are involved when you're talking

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.990
about the creation and
publication of music.

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:29.540
Music is particularly complex

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:31.010
when it comes to copyright law,

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:32.120
so for simplicity's sake,

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:34.390
we're just gonna talk
about the two main ones

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:35.223
when it comes to creation.

00:04:35.223 --> 00:04:37.240
There's the musical work copyright

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:39.570
and there is the sound
recording copyright.

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:40.890
But what rights are these?

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:43.590
Under the Copyright
Act 17 USC section 106,

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:45.150
the owner of a copyright in a work

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:46.730
has certain exclusive rights to that work,

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:49.070
including the rights to
reproduce or copy that work,

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:51.370
adapt and prepare derivative
works based on the original,

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:54.230
distribute copies of it,
perform or display it publicly,

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:55.860
perform a sound recording publicly

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:58.550
by digital audio transmissions, et cetera.

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:01.210
And how long do these
exclusive rights last?

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:03.170
Well, since your songs were created

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:06.730
on or after January 1st,
1978, they last for 70 years

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:09.000
after the death of the individual author.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:10.890
So let's talk about this first right,

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:14.380
the music composition right
or the musical work copyright.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:16.630
It protects the underlying
musical arrangement

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:18.700
and the accompanying lyrics of a song,

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:20.500
whether that composition is fixed

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:23.060
in written sheet music,
recorded, or otherwise.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:25.210
And in our hypothetical,
you created a musical work

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:27.320
all by yourself, so you own that copyright

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:29.160
until you sign the contract assigning it

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:32.070
to the Screaming Eagle
Music Publishing Company.

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:33.450
And under our proposed agreement,

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:36.110
I will let you keep 50% of
the music composition rights.

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:38.330
But that takes us to the second copyright:

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:40.330
the sound recording copyright,

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:42.250
which protects the actual recording

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:43.460
of a musical composition.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:45.650
This could be a recording
that's contained in a CD,

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:50.060
an MP3 file, a computer hard
drive, or other phonorecords.

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:53.550
The Copyright Act needs
to update its language.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:55.570
But the Copyright Act defines phonorecords

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:58.737
as material objects
which sound is affixed to

00:05:58.737 --> 00:06:01.430
"by any method now known
or later developed,"

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:02.840
and under this proposed contract

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:04.740
with Screaming Eagle Records,

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:06.850
the label will pay for
all of the musicians

00:06:06.850 --> 00:06:08.510
and the equipment that you
need to record an album.

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:10.150
The official finished sound recordings

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:12.030
will be owned by the record company.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:15.040
This is the version of the
song that is publicly released.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:17.120
These are generally
called master recordings,

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:19.960
and Screaming Eagle Records
will not need your permission

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:22.690
now or in the future to use
these particular records

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:23.790
or to sell them.

00:06:23.790 --> 00:06:25.630
How valuable are the masters?

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:28.370
So valuable that everyone
wants a piece of this pie.

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:30.150
It's become a popular
practice for producers

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:32.230
and song engineers to demand a percentage

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:34.270
of the masters for the work of their song.

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:36.080
And sometimes this is an
advantageous arrangement

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:37.660
for your musicians, but be careful,

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:39.710
because it will keep
eating into your split

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:43.100
any time that recording
is played somewhere else.

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:44.500
So to recap, under our deal,

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:47.110
which would not be totally
crazy in the music industry,

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:49.150
Screaming Eagle owns your masters,

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:51.920
but you get to keep 50%
of the composition rights.

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:54.540
So this seems like a pretty
reasonable deal, right?

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:55.373
Or is it?

00:06:55.373 --> 00:06:56.400
Music publishing is not something

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:58.470
that's defined by the copyright statute.

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:01.110
It's a music industry
tradition and expectation

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:04.040
that a music publisher will
manage the composition rights.

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:06.430
Music publishers help leverage
your songs to make money

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:08.850
and protect your catalog
of songs from infringement.

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:09.690
And generally speaking,

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:12.440
music publishers have two
significant revenue streams.

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:15.080
There are mechanical royalties
and performance royalties.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:16.750
A music publisher can pitch your song

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:18.590
to other artists who may record them,

00:07:18.590 --> 00:07:20.810
or they can get music
plugged into advertisements,

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:22.270
video games, movies, and the like.

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:23.870
This might be financially lucrative,

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:25.680
but it still means that as the musician,

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:28.120
you're giving up another
slice of the overall pie.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:30.950
So when will the individual
artist start earning money?

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:32.130
Well, first, you've got to ensure

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:35.160
that you sell enough albums to
make up the $100,000 advance

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:37.650
that you signed when you
signed with the label.

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:39.930
That money was an advance,
not a signing bonus.

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:41.580
And then you have to repay the music label

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:43.830
for any expenses it paid
to help record your album,

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:45.200
promote it, and set up your tour.

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:47.870
TLC only got about 20 cents per album

00:07:47.870 --> 00:07:50.860
after the label deducted for
dancers, sound engineers,

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:53.540
video production, tour
support, and radio promotion.

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:55.890
Left Eye said that the
group made less than 1%

00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:58.970
of the estimated $175 million in revenue

00:07:58.970 --> 00:08:00.350
that the group's music generated.

00:08:00.350 --> 00:08:01.430
So let that be a lesson:

00:08:01.430 --> 00:08:03.940
only after you pay back the label in full

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:06.230
will you start earning
royalties on your music.

00:08:06.230 --> 00:08:07.680
But once the contract between you

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:09.780
and the first music label ends,

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:12.130
you might be able to sign a
more lucrative deal elsewhere.

00:08:12.130 --> 00:08:15.160
However, the original music
label will own the masters

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:17.200
of the sound recordings in your albums

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:19.600
for as long as it states in the contract,

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:21.730
which could be as long as
your life and then some.

00:08:21.730 --> 00:08:23.360
The label will continue to get revenue

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:25.700
for the sale of each
album and digital download

00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:28.090
that includes any of the
original sound recordings,

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:29.810
and that includes royalties or fees

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:32.130
from the use of the sound
records in any movies,

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:34.070
films, TV shows, or advertisements.

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:36.000
And now you know enough
about copyright law

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.370
and the music industry to
understand what happened

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:40.600
with Taylor Swift and Big Machine.

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:42.370
Imagine that you signed a deal like this

00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:44.420
when you were 15 years
old, like Taylor Swift.

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:46.920
In 2019, Swift was named
Artist of the Decade

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:50.120
for the six albums she made
between the ages of 15 and 24,

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:52.840
mostly while she was signed to
the record label Big Machine.

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:56.270
And not surprisingly, this
soon erupted to bad blood.

00:08:56.270 --> 00:08:57.940
But the trouble began when Scooter Braun,

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:00.640
who works with the Kardashian
clan and Justin Bieber,

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:02.540
purchased the music label Big Machine.

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:04.990
Braun and Swift had
publicly feuded in the past,

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:06.100
and once the sale went through,

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:08.680
that meant that Swift's
masters were owned by Braun.

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:10.360
Big Machine was estimated to be worth

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:13.300
at least $300 million with
Swift's catalog valued

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:16.520
at about $140 million at the
time, so she was responsible

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:18.040
for almost half of the company's value.

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:19.977
But this was not a love story.

00:09:19.977 --> 00:09:22.023
♪ Baby, just say ♪

00:09:22.023 --> 00:09:23.723
- No! God!

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:27.080
No, God, please, no.

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:28.210
- When her contract was up,

00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:30.190
Swift opted not to
resign with the company,

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:32.520
but Big Machine was not exactly thrilled

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:34.280
with her decision to
sign with another label.

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:36.480
So she says that they
retaliated against her

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:39.260
by refusing to allow her
to perform her songs on TV

00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:41.400
and blocking her from using her recordings

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:43.360
in a Netflix documentary about her life.

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:44.407
Although the label denied this,

00:09:44.407 --> 00:09:46.640
"The New York Times" got an
email showing an executive

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:48.890
from Big Machine telling
Swift's representatives

00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:50.017
that the company will, quote,

00:09:50.017 --> 00:09:52.550
"not agree to issue licenses
for existing records

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:54.970
or waivers of its recording restrictions

00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:57.370
in connection with the
Netflix documentary."

00:09:57.370 --> 00:09:59.310
Yes, Taylor brought the receipts.

00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:02.236
The lesson: don't second-guess a madwoman.

00:10:02.236 --> 00:10:05.604
♪ Baby, now we got bad blood, hey ♪

00:10:05.604 --> 00:10:07.823
(sighs) Oh, boy. (laughs)

00:10:07.823 --> 00:10:10.820
Now, Swift tried to buy her
masters from Big Machine,

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:13.430
but Braun refused to sell
them to her at any price.

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:15.160
Braun wanted Swift to sign an NDA

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:16.820
muzzling her right to talk about him

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:18.500
as a condition of negotiating.

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:19.780
Swift said no deal.

00:10:19.780 --> 00:10:21.460
But this is a dilemma
faced by many artists

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:23.060
who try to buy back their copyrights.

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:25.690
In the late 1960s, Paul
McCartney lost his stake

00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:27.290
in Northern Songs, the publishing company

00:10:27.290 --> 00:10:28.580
that he'd set up with John Lennon.

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:30.040
McCartney also lost control

00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:31.840
over the master recordings
for the Beatles.

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:34.470
And when McCartney worked with
Michael Jackson in the 1980s,

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:36.070
he told Jackson that it was important

00:10:36.070 --> 00:10:37.950
to own the rights to his own music.

00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:40.597
Jackson apparently jokingly
responded by saying,

00:10:40.597 --> 00:10:42.750
"One day, I'll own all of your music."

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:44.230
And, mission accomplished!

00:10:44.230 --> 00:10:46.210
Jackson responded to this advice

00:10:46.210 --> 00:10:49.120
by buying up the McCartney
copyrights for himself

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:51.950
in a backstabby joint
venture with Sony ATV

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:54.370
that cost him $47 million.

00:10:54.370 --> 00:10:58.010
- For those of us who
like living dangerously,

00:10:58.010 --> 00:10:59.320
this one's for you.

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:01.200
- McCartney, of course,
got nothing from the deal.

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:02.550
It also, shocker, apparently,

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:04.430
ruined the McCartney-Jackson friendship.

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:06.470
Now, the value of the
Beatles' catalog soared.

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:09.600
In 2016, Sony ATV bought the Jackson share

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:13.780
of the Beatles' songs for
a whopping $750 million.

00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:15.860
But after years of litigation
over whether British

00:11:15.860 --> 00:11:18.100
or American copyright
law applied to the sale,

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:20.500
McCartney finally got the
chance to get his rights back

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:22.700
using the termination
provisions of the Copyright Act,

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:24.820
which generally gives
an artist an opportunity

00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:27.820
to terminate transfers 35 years
after the date of transfer.

00:11:27.820 --> 00:11:29.670
But what did this mean for Taylor Swift?

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:32.690
Well, it meant that she decided
to be proactive right now

00:11:32.690 --> 00:11:34.980
to avoid ever being in
a McCartney situation,

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:38.250
which brings us to why
she rerecorded her albums.

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:41.340
17 months after Scooter
Braun purchased Big Machine,

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:43.760
he sold the master tapes of Swift's albums

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:45.200
to a private investment fund.

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:48.260
The purchase price was a
minimum of $300 million,

00:11:48.260 --> 00:11:50.640
which could escalate to $450 million

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:52.240
if certain benchmarks were met.

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:54.610
The investment funds have
been scooping up music rights

00:11:54.610 --> 00:11:56.830
left and right because
their value is so high.

00:11:56.830 --> 00:11:58.140
For example, in 2020,

00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:01.590
a firm purchased a 50% stake
in Rick James's catalog,

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:04.550
including both publishing
and recorded masters.

00:12:04.550 --> 00:12:05.910
The song "Super Freak" is still one

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:07.710
of the most sampled riffs in music

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:09.940
and is still probably worth
tens of millions of dollars,

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:10.773
if not more.

00:12:10.773 --> 00:12:12.460
But when James died in 2003,

00:12:12.460 --> 00:12:15.640
he left an estate worth just $250,000.

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:17.830
The investment firm that
purchased the Swift masters

00:12:17.830 --> 00:12:19.580
includes the George Soros family,

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:21.520
23 Capital, and the Carlyle Group.

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:23.680
According to Swift, they have
not reached out to her at all,

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:25.400
which is a strange business decision,

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:27.270
since Swift is making them money.

00:12:27.270 --> 00:12:29.160
Swift wrote, "Scott Borchetta told my team

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:30.520
that they'll allow me to use my music

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:32.600
only if I do these things: if I agree

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:35.370
not to rerecord copycat
versions of my songs next year,

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:37.670
which is something I'm
both legally allowed to do

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:40.030
and looking forward to,
and also told my team

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:42.470
that I need to stop talking
about him and Scooter Braun."

00:12:42.470 --> 00:12:44.900
Braun's response to Swift's
specific allegations

00:12:44.900 --> 00:12:47.520
was basically-
♪ You need to calm down ♪

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:50.010
He posted about kindness on
Instagram and had the gall

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:52.390
to assert that Taylor
communicating with her fans,

00:12:52.390 --> 00:12:54.120
quote, "greatly affects the safety

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:55.880
of our employees and their families."

00:12:55.880 --> 00:12:58.940
So Swift just responded by
re-recording her original albums,

00:12:58.940 --> 00:12:59.773
because remember,

00:12:59.773 --> 00:13:01.910
there's two different
copyrights at work here.

00:13:01.910 --> 00:13:03.900
There is the composition license,

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:06.420
the sheet music, the melodies, the lyrics,

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:08.000
and then there is the copyright

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:09.750
in the sound recording itself.

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:12.230
She didn't own the copyright
in the sound recording.

00:13:12.230 --> 00:13:14.960
Big Machine and eventually
the private equity firms did.

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:16.640
But she did own the composition license,

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:20.720
so she just took the
compositions and rerecorded them.

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:23.760
Effectively, Swift owned the
abstract version of the songs,

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:25.300
and she had the funds available

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:28.230
to work in a studio to
rerecord it herself.

00:13:28.230 --> 00:13:30.830
She could hire the engineers
and the other musicians

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:33.440
to help out on the album,
and she could just make one

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:35.960
that sounded identical
to the original album.

00:13:35.960 --> 00:13:36.793
You might ask,

00:13:36.793 --> 00:13:39.350
how does this re-recording
impact the original masters

00:13:39.350 --> 00:13:41.380
that are now owned by
a private equity firm?

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:43.260
Well, it dilutes their values.

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:45.490
Fans are buying the new
version of the album.

00:13:45.490 --> 00:13:48.120
In fact, it went to #1
on the Billboard charts.

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:50.650
It also means that fans will
stream the new versions,

00:13:50.650 --> 00:13:53.160
reducing the number of plays
that the original version gets

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:54.630
on streaming services.

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:57.470
And any company that wants to
license Taylor Swift's albums

00:13:57.470 --> 00:14:00.530
can now work directly with
Swift, which cuts off income

00:14:00.530 --> 00:14:02.490
to the firm that owns
the original masters.

00:14:02.490 --> 00:14:03.870
And you might think that
the two different versions

00:14:03.870 --> 00:14:05.880
of the sound recordings might
compete with each other,

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:08.570
driving the price down, but in reality,

00:14:08.570 --> 00:14:11.360
people generally wanna work
with the original artist

00:14:11.360 --> 00:14:14.550
because that artist is going
to continue making new art

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:16.810
that they might want to
license in the future.

00:14:16.810 --> 00:14:18.310
It's generally better for a movie,

00:14:18.310 --> 00:14:20.470
game, or TV show to
license the new version

00:14:20.470 --> 00:14:21.970
because they would have
a good relationship

00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:24.660
with Taylor Swift, who has an
entire career in front of her.

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:27.900
So Taylor Swift made extremely
important business decisions

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:30.610
that will not only impact her,
but other artists as well.

00:14:30.610 --> 00:14:32.560
The private firm that
owns the original albums

00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:34.280
also probably missed an opportunity.

00:14:34.280 --> 00:14:37.390
If they had reached out to
smooth things over with Swift,

00:14:37.390 --> 00:14:39.690
the value of the original
masters would be intact.

00:14:39.690 --> 00:14:41.810
Instead, her fans are
buying the new album,

00:14:41.810 --> 00:14:43.850
which sounds identical to the original.

00:14:43.850 --> 00:14:46.140
Swift plans to rerecord her
other five albums as well,

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:47.790
and if Big Machine is bitter,

00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:49.680
I guess that they can just shake it off.

